`items` is `undefined` when `.map` runs on it. This is the standard cause of this error.

Minimal code that throws it:

```jsx
// List.tsx
function List({ items }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}
    </ul>
  );
}

// Usage: no items prop, no default
<List />
```

Line 4 runs `items.map` on `undefined` and throws `TypeError: cannot read property 'map' of undefined`.

Common triggers for this in real code:
- A parent passes no prop, or passes `null`.
- Data comes from an API call and renders before the response arrives.
- A prior state update sets the array to `undefined` instead of `[]`.

Fix — give `items` a default value:

```jsx
function List({ items = [] }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

If the value can be `null` too (default values do not catch `null`), guard the call instead:

```jsx
{items?.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>) ?? null}
```

